--TEST--
`Core\Json::decodeAs<array<T>>` reads a JSON array as one `T` per element, with the element's position on every issue it reports — `rule:core-classes/derive-field-list` and `rule:core-classes/derive-reports-every-field`, `rule:types/grammar`
--FILE--
<?nvs
#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Point {
    public int $x;
    #[Core\Json\Field(name: "label")]
    public string $tag;

    public function constructor(int $x, string $tag) {
        $this->x = $x;
        $this->tag = $tag;
    }
}

// § 2: a decode is an ordinary `new`, so a list decode is that `new` run once
// per element — the type argument names the element's class and the result is
// an `array<Point>`, not the `array<mixed>` a plain `decode` answers with.
var $points = Core\Json::decodeAs<array<Point>>(
    "[{\"x\":1,\"label\":\"a\"},{\"x\":2,\"label\":\"b\"},{\"x\":3,\"label\":\"c\"}]"
);
echo "count=", Core\Arr::count($points), "\n";

// The elements are `Point`s to the checker as well as at run time: a binding
// declaring the element type is what proves the type argument reached the
// result, since `array<mixed>` would refuse this loop at compile time.
var $sum = 0;
foreach ($points as Point $point) {
    $sum = $sum + $point->x;
    echo "  ", $point->tag, "=", $point->x, "\n";
}
echo "sum=", $sum, "\n";

// The document's own order, and the same document back: a list that decoded
// element by element re-encodes to the bytes it came from.
echo "round=", Core\Json::encode($points), "\n";

// The empty list is the bound on the near side — nothing to construct is not
// a failure to construct.
echo "empty=", Core\Arr::count(Core\Json::decodeAs<array<Point>>("[]")), "\n";

// § 5 accumulates within an element and the list stops at the first bad one:
// element 1 reports both of its own failures, element 2's missing field is
// never reached, and every path carries the position. A list refused anywhere
// is refused entirely — a shorter `array<Point>` than the document held would
// be invisible to the caller.
try {
    Core\Json::decodeAs<array<Point>>(
        "[{\"x\":1,\"label\":\"a\"},{\"x\":\"two\"},{\"x\":3}]"
    );
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $bad) {
    echo "issues=", Core\Arr::count($bad->issues), "\n";
    foreach ($bad->issues as {path: string, message: string} $issue) {
        echo "  bad=", $issue->path, "\n";
    }
}

// The far side of the same bound: a JSON *object* is what `decodeAs<T>` reads,
// so it is not an `array<T>`, and the refusal names the shape rather than
// reporting every field of the class as missing.
try {
    Core\Json::decodeAs<array<Point>>("{\"x\":1,\"label\":\"a\"}");
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $object) {
    echo "object=", $object->issues["0"]->message, "\n";
}

// An element that is not an object at all is named by its position, which is
// the one thing a list decode knows that a single decode does not.
try {
    Core\Json::decodeAs<array<Point>>("[{\"x\":1,\"label\":\"a\"},7]");
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $scalar) {
    echo "scalar=", $scalar->issues["0"]->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
count=3
  a=1
  b=2
  c=3
sum=6
round=[{"x":1,"label":"a"},{"x":2,"label":"b"},{"x":3,"label":"c"}]
empty=0
issues=2
  bad=1.x
  bad=1.label
object=Core\Json::decodeAs(): an `array<Point>` decodes from a JSON array
scalar=Core\Json::decodeAs(): element 1 is not a JSON object, and a `Point` decodes from one
